{"step_index":43,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T12:39:35Z","input_tokens":3478,"cache_read_tokens":73994,"output_tokens":1446,"thinking":"Since the ADM is a closed native binary that restarts on every CoreAudio device notification, I should think about what the app itself can realistically do—probably start with cheap diagnostics like logging device updates and engine restarts with timestamps to confirm the flapping pattern before considering an actual fix.\n\nI'm also questioning whether the 4-6 reported interruptions can really be tied to a single logged engine restart, or whether some come from macOS itself reconfiguring the audio route (BT HFP transitions, sample-rate switches) rather than the ADM. I think the honest answer is to lay out what's provable from the logs versus what's speculative, then propose the diagnostic logging and possible mitigations and ask whether to proceed with implementation.\n\nI should propose adding probe logging around device updates and engine lifecycle events with uptime and device names so the next repro pins down exact counts and causes. For the immediate fix, I can skip the native mute call when the module is already muted and talk isn't unmuted, avoiding the blocking timeout during rebuild — though that doesn't address the root interruption cause, which really lives in debouncing ADM's default-device notifications.\n\nSince features like platformAudioProcessingState and engineAvailability look like custom fork additions rather than stock WebRTC, I should check the Dependencies directory to confirm whether we're on a forked WebRTC build before deciding where the debounce logic should live.\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"cd /Users/v.smirnov/docs/conference-guest-ios && ls Dependencies; git grep -n -i 'webrtc' -- project.yml Package.swift '*/Package.swift' 2>/dev/null | head","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolSummary":"Check WebRTC dependency source"}}]}
