{"step_index":47,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T12:39:56Z","input_tokens":1599,"cache_read_tokens":78918,"output_tokens":1344,"content":"I checked your diagnostics bundle and the audio code. This bundle can't show what caused each of the 4–6 interruptions, because the app doesn't log device changes or engine restarts as separate events. Here's what it does show.\n\n## What the logs show\n\n- **The switch happened at about 12:35:45.** That's when the app's recovery step tried to mute the mic. That step only runs when the audio engine stops on its own. You had been muted since 12:00:41, so you didn't trigger it.\n- **That mute call hung for more than 2 seconds and timed out** at 12:35:47 (`physical-audio-operation-timeout`, `set-microphone-muted`). It was most likely stuck waiting while the audio library threw away its engine and built a new one, which is slow with echo cancellation on.\n- **The audio counters from that moment** are the only ones in the bundle:\n\n  | counter | value |\n  |---|---|\n  | `deviceUpdates` | 3 |\n  | `enginesCreated` / `enginesReleased` | 2 / 1 |\n  | `engineStarts` / `engineStops` | 2 / 1 |\n\n  So 2 seconds in, macOS had already sent 3 device-change notices and the engine had been fully rebuilt once.\n- **Nothing was recorded after 12:35:47.** The engine-restart and device-change callbacks only bump counters in memory ([GuestSystemAudio.swift#L1053-L1161](file:///Users/v.smirnov/docs/conference-guest-ios/Experiments/NativeGuest/GuestSystemAudio.swift#L1053-L1161)). The device list in `report.txt` is from 12:36:07, but its audio counters are just a copy of that same 12:35:47 event. Any later rebuilds that might explain interruptions 2–6 can't be seen.\n\n## Likely cause (not confirmed)\n\nMoving from AirPods to the built-in speakers and mic makes macOS send several separate notices: output changed, input changed, AirPods dropped off the device list, and the speakers run at 44.1 kHz. The audio layer comes from your own LiveKit WebRTC build, which ships compiled, so I couldn't read how it reacts to these notices. The counters fit a pattern where it rebuilds the engine once per notice instead of waiting for things to settle. Each rebuild would cause a dropout, and that would add up to the 4–6 you heard.\n\nThe app's recovery adds to the pile-up. On each engine stop, [`engineChanged(.stopped)`](file:///Users/v.smirnov/docs/conference-guest-ios/NativeMacGuest/Engines/SharedAudioEngine.swift#L333-L355) queues a blocking mute call into the audio library, even when you're already muted. When that times out, `suppressMicrophone()` queues another one. These waits block the audio library and could make recovery slower.\n\n## Proposed fixes\n\n1. **Logging (small, low risk):** write a timestamped event for each device-change notice and each engine create, start, stop and release, with the current system input and output device names (the existing helper in `GuestAcousticEnvironment` already reads these). The next time this happens, the bundle would show every rebuild and what caused it.\n2. **App-side change (small):** in `engineChanged(.stopped)`, skip the native mute call when the mic is already muted (`moduleMuted && !talkUnmuted`). Turning off the senders stays immediate. This removes the 2-second timeout and the extra queued call.\n3. **The real fix, in the WebRTC build:** collect device-change notices for about 300–500 ms and rebuild the engine once, after the input and output devices stop changing. This needs a new `vpio-private` release.\n\nShould I do 1 and 2 now? After that, one more AirPods → built-in switch would tell us whether 3 is needed and how many rebuilds actually happen.","thinking":"The ADM device-change handling isn't checked in as source here, just patches for the livekit Swift layer—that's enough to go on. I'll present the diagnosis and proposal now, then ask whether to implement steps 1 and 2.\n\n"}
